(Title) Tj
T*
(Outcomes of Onsite Telemedicine-Facilitated Hepatitis C Treatment Integrated Within Opioid) Tj
T*
(Treatment Programs: Effectiveness and Patient Satisfaction Results) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Abstract) Tj
T*
(Treatment barriers reduce hepatitis C cure rates in opioid-using populations. We conducted) Tj
T*
(a hybrid effectiveness-implementation analysis comparing telemedicine provision within) Tj
T*
(opioid treatment programs \(OTPs\) versus standard referral to offsite hepatology. The) Tj
T*
(integration approach involved on-site clinical coordinators, telemedicine consultation) Tj
T*
(infrastructure, and care coordination. Among participants receiving telemedicine within) Tj
T*
(their OTP, hepatitis C viral clearance was achieved in 81% \(95% CI 76-85%\) compared with) Tj
T*
(64% \(95% CI 58-70%\) among the referral group. Treatment completion rates were 89% in the) Tj
T*
(integrated model versus 71% in the referral model. Patient satisfaction with healthcare) Tj
T*
(provider contact duration was significantly higher in the telemedicine group \(mean 7.4 vs.) Tj
T*
(5.1 on 10-point scale, p<0.001\). Organizational factors associated with superior) Tj
T*
(effectiveness included on-site care coordination staff consistency, manageable clinical) Tj
T*
(provider-to-patient ratios, and proactive participant identification. Telemedicine-enabled) Tj
T*
(co-location of hepatitis C services with substance use treatment substantially improved) Tj
T*
(both virologic outcomes and participant satisfaction.) Tj
